{
  "name": "Roofs",
  "_about": "THE COMMON ROOFS, all one part type (`roof`) because every one of them is a stack of rectangles joined side to side -- the eave, any break line, the ridge -- and a side that comes in between two levels is a SLOPE while one that does not is a GABLE. In the front row, on the same 6 x 9m house: FLAT, SHED (lean-to), GABLE, HIP, PYRAMID (pavilion), MANSARD (steep all round, then a low hip), GAMBREL (the barn's: the same on the long sides only), JERKINHEAD (a gable with its top hipped back -- the clipped gable), GABLET (a hip with a small gable over it -- the Dutch gable) and SALTBOX (a gable with its ridge off the middle). `pitch` is in degrees; `overhang` carries the eave out and down its own slope and the verge out over the gable. Behind them, the TOWER ROOFS, which are domes of `thickness` 0 -- a skin -- struck from arch profiles: the cone (trigonal), the octagonal spire, the onion, the bell (ogee), the flared spire (inflexed) and the tented roof of a square tower. `sides` matches the tower's own bays, so the roof's corners stand on the tower's.",
